@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        It is confessed by all divines that upon the words of the consecration, the body and blood of 
        Christ is really and substantially present, and so exhibited and given to all that receive it; 
        and all this not after a physical and sensual, but after a heavenly and incomprehensible manner.
    </span>
    <span>Bishop John Overall, Commentary on the Book of Common Prayer</span>
</div>

<ol>
    <li>
        In the Lord's Supper, or the Eucharist, all the great benefits of Christ's atoning death and resurrection
        are presented again to His church, so its members are bound to Christ and to each other in holy communion. 
    </li>
    <li>
        By partaking of the Eucharist in faith, a person is engrafted into Christ, forgiven of his sins, renewed as a
        member of Christ's body and covenant, and freed from the powers of sin and death.
    </li>
    <li>
        These mighty benefits are effected because the Eucharist is not mere bread, but a holy sacrament that 
        unites bread to Christ's resurrected body which resides in heaven. This union is not physical or local, but
        heavenly, spiritual, and mysterious, and is wrought by the promises of God through
        the work of the Holy Spirit.
    </li>
    <li>
        Because the promises of God are true and must not be doubted, Christ is offered and exhibitied in the 
        Eucharist just as surely as we see the bread before our eyes. In this way Christ is truly present in the
        Eucharist under the form of bread and wine.
    </li>
    <li>
        The presence of Christ in the sacrament does not depend on the condition of those present, but only upon the
        promises of God that are invoked through the words of consecration. Christ is doubtless received
        and experienced by all who partake, though His body is only eaten unto salvation
        for those who receive it by faith.
    </li>
    <li>
        Any who partake unworthily without faith will not partake in the blessings of Christ as his sheep
        do, but will perceive Him as a terrible judge who bears the sword against all ungodliness.
        As Saint Paul teaches, they will receive judgement and condemnation.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />